## `n_before_user` and checkpoint behavior in beellama

**`n_before_user` does not exist in beellama.** It was referenced in the working record from an upstream context but is absent in this fork. However, checkpoint behavior is present and well-developed. Here's how it works:

### Configuration

From `common/common.h`:
```cpp
int32_t n_ctx_checkpoints   = 32;    // max number of context checkpoints per slot
int32_t checkpoint_min_step = 8192;  // minimum spacing between context checkpoints
```

### The checkpoint struct (`common/common.h:1214`)

```cpp
struct common_prompt_checkpoint {
    int64_t n_tokens;
    int id_task = -1;              // which task created this checkpoint
    llama_pos pos_min;
    llama_pos pos_max;
    std::vector<uint8_t> data_tgt; // target model KV cache snapshot
    std::vector<uint8_t> data_dft; // draft model KV cache snapshot
    std::vector<uint8_t> data_spec; // speculative decoder internal state
    // ... update_tgt, update_dft, load_tgt, load_dft, clear, size, etc.
};
```

Each checkpoint stores a **partial KV cache snapshot** for both the target and draft (speculative) contexts, plus the speculative decoder's internal state.

### Creation (`server-context.cpp:2516`)

```cpp
void create_checkpoint(server_slot & slot, const int64_t n_tokens_cur, llama_pos pos_min, llama_pos pos_max) {
    const int id_task = slot.task->id;

    // evict checkpoints within min-step of a previous checkpoint, unless they were
    // created by the current task
    int64_t last = -1;
    for (auto it = slot.prompt.checkpoints.begin(); it != slot.prompt.checkpoints.end(); ) {
        if (it->id_task != id_task && last >= 0 && it->n_tokens <= last + params_base.checkpoint_min_step) {
            SLT_TRC(slot, "erasing context checkpoint too close to an earlier one ...\n", ...);
            it = slot.prompt.checkpoints.erase(it);
            continue;
        }
        last = it->n_tokens;
        ++it;
    }

    // evict oldest if at capacity
    while (slot.prompt.checkpoints.size() >= (size_t) params_base.n_ctx_checkpoints) {
        slot.prompt.checkpoints.erase(slot.prompt.checkpoints.begin());
    }

    auto & cur = slot.prompt.checkpoints.emplace_back();
    cur.id_task = id_task;
    cur.update_pos(slot.prompt.n_tokens() - n_tokens_cur, pos_min, pos_max);
    cur.update_tgt(ctx_tgt, slot.id, LLAMA_STATE_SEQ_FLAGS_PARTIAL_ONLY);
    cur.update_dft(ctx_dft, slot.id, LLAMA_STATE_SEQ_FLAGS_PARTIAL_ONLY);
    common_speculative_get_state(spec.get(), slot.id, cur.data_spec);
}
```

Checkpoints are created at user message boundaries or near the end of prompts (not mid-ASSISTANT), spaced at least `checkpoint_min_step` tokens apart, capped at `n_ctx_checkpoints` per slot.

### Restoration during rollback (`server-context.cpp:3585`)

When a new request shares a prefix with an existing slot, the server searches for a viable checkpoint:

```cpp
const auto it = std::find_if(
    slot.prompt.checkpoints.rbegin(),
    slot.prompt.checkpoints.rend(),
    [&](const auto & cur) {
        SLT_TRC(slot, "checking checkpoint with [%d, %d] against %d...\n", cur.pos_min, cur.pos_max, pos_min_thold);
        if (cur.pos_max > pos_next) {
            return false;
        }
        return cur.pos_min < pos_min_thold || cur.pos_min == 0;
    }
);

if (!do_reset) {
    it->load_tgt(ctx_tgt, slot.id, LLAMA_STATE_SEQ_FLAGS_PARTIAL_ONLY);
    // ... restore draft and speculative state too
    SLT_TRC(slot, "restored context checkpoint (pos_min = %d, pos_max = %d, n_tokens = %" PRId64 ")\n", ...);
}
```

After a context shift (when the KV cache is full and tokens are discarded), invalidated checkpoints are erased:

```cpp
// erase any checkpoints with pos_max > pos_next
for (auto it = slot.prompt.checkpoints.begin(); it != slot.prompt.checkpoints.end();) {
    if (cur.pos_max > pos_next) {
        SLT_TRC(slot, "erased invalidated context checkpoint ...\n", ...);
        it = slot.prompt.checkpoints.erase(it);
    } else {
        ++it;
    }
}
```

### Summary

Checkpoints in beellama are **in-memory partial KV cache snapshots** stored per-slot at user-message boundaries. They enable fast rollback when a new request shares a prompt prefix — instead of re-processing the shared prefix from scratch, the server restores the saved KV state and only decodes the divergent suffix. They are automatically pruned by spacing (`checkpoint_min_step`), capacity (`n_ctx_checkpoints`), and invalidated by context shifts. The speculative decoder state (`data_spec`) is also checkpointed so draft-model state survives restore.

