## 4. Caching and Checkpointing Differences Between Upstream and the Fork (beellama)

The beellama fork diverges from upstream llama.cpp in several subtle but important ways around prompt caching and context checkpointing. These differences affect when checkpoints are created, how they are gated, and what metadata drives checkpoint decisions.

### 4.1 Checkpoint Gating: `n_before_user` vs `spans.is_user_start()`

**Upstream** uses turn-span tracking (`spans.is_user_start()` and `is_last_user_message`) to decide whether to create checkpoints at user-turn boundaries:

```cpp
// upstream: tools/server/server-context.cpp:3528-3547
const bool is_user_start = spans.is_user_start(n_tokens_start);
const bool is_last_user_message = n_tokens_start == last_user_pos;
// ...
// skip ordinary mid-prompt checkpoints, unless the batch starts a user
// message or we are near the end of the prompt
if (!is_user_start && !near_prompt_end) {
    do_checkpoint = false;
}
```

**Beellama** replaces this with an explicit `n_before_user` field in `task_params` (`tools/server/server-task.h:66`), which stores the token position just before the user message:

```cpp
// beellama: tools/server/server-task.h:66
int32_t n_before_user = -1;

// beellama: tools/server/server-context.cpp:3135-3136
const int32_t n_before_user = slot.task->params.n_before_user;
const bool n_before_user_known = n_before_user > 0;
```

The checkpoint suppression logic in the fork uses `n_before_user_known` instead of `is_user_start`:

```cpp
// beellama: tools/server/server-context.cpp:3215
if (!n_before_user_known && !near_prompt_end) {
    do_checkpoint = false;
}
```

And adds an additional `is_allowed` gate that restricts checkpoints to exactly two positions relative to `n_before_user`:

```cpp
// beellama: tools/server/server-context.cpp:3227-3243
const bool is_on_user =
    n_before_user_known &&
    n_tokens_start == n_before_user;

const bool is_after_user =
    n_before_user_known &&
    n_tokens_start > n_before_user;

const bool is_allowed =
    !n_before_user_known ||
    is_on_user ||
    (is_after_user && near_prompt_end);

if (do_checkpoint && !is_allowed) {
    do_checkpoint = false;
}
```

This means when `n_before_user` is known, checkpoints are only created either **exactly at** the user boundary (`is_on_user`) or **after** the user boundary but near the prompt end (`is_after_user && near_prompt_end`). Mid-prompt checkpoints between user turns are suppressed.

### 4.2 Prompt Batch Break Logic: Conditional vs Unconditional

**Upstream** breaks the prompt batch at every user message start unconditionally:

```cpp
// upstream: tools/server/server-context.cpp:3494
// stop the prompt batch exactly before a user message
if (spans.is_user_start(slot.prompt.n_tokens())) {
    break;
}
```

**Beellama** makes this break conditional on checkpointing being enabled and `n_before_user` being known:

```cpp
// beellama: tools/server/server-context.cpp:3168-3171
// stop the prompt batch exactly before the latest user input, so a checkpoint
// can be created after the previous messages
if (n_before_user_known &&
    slot.prompt.n_tokens() == n_before_user) {
    break;
}
```

This means upstream always creates batch boundaries at user turns, while beellama only does so when checkpointing is relevant — potentially producing different batch sizes and timing characteristics.

### 4.3 Checkpoint Spacing Policy: `is_last_user_message` vs `near_prompt_end`

**Upstream** exempts the last user message position from the `checkpoint_min_step` spacing requirement:

```cpp
// upstream: tools/server/server-context.cpp:3565
do_checkpoint = do_checkpoint && (slot.prompt.checkpoints.empty() || is_last_user_message || n_tokens_start > slot.prompt.checkpoints.back().n_tokens + params_base.checkpoint_min_step);
```

**Beellama** does not have an `is_last_user_message` exemption in the spacing policy. Instead, the `near_prompt_end` override from the `is_allowed` gate serves a similar but not identical function — it allows checkpoints near the end of the prompt rather than specifically at the last user message position.

### 4.4 Prompt Cache: Consumed Entries (Shared Behavior)

Both upstream and beellama share the same prompt cache consumption model: `get_available_slot()` in `server-task.cpp` calls `load()` which finds the best matching cache entry and **erases** it from the cache after moving it into the slot (`states.erase(it_best)`). This single-use cache entry behavior is identical in both codebases.

### 4.5 Context Checkpoint Storage and SWA Limitation (Shared)

Both upstream and beellama share the same `create_checkpoint()` implementation and the same documented SWA limitation:

```cpp
// both: tools/server/server-context.cpp (create_checkpoint)
// TODO: here we incorrectly deterimne that the saved checkpoint data covers the [pos_min, pos_max] range
// this is not true for SWA models.
```

Checkpoint state includes main context (`data_tgt`), draft/speculative context (`data_dft`), and speculative decoder state (`data_spec`), and both codebases store checkpoints in `slot.prompt.checkpoints` with LRU eviction when `n_ctx_checkpoints` is exceeded.

### Summary of Divergences

| Aspect | Upstream | Beellama Fork |
|--------|----------|---------------|
| Turn-boundary detection | `spans.is_user_start()` + `is_last_user_message` | `n_before_user` field in `task_params` |
| Batch break at user turns | Unconditional | Conditional on `n_before_user_known` |
| Mid-prompt checkpoint suppression | `!is_user_start && !near_prompt_end` | `!n_before_user_known && !near_prompt_end` |
| Position-specific gate | None (any batch boundary can checkpoint) | `is_allowed` gate: only at `n_before_user` or after+near_end |
| Spacing exemption | `is_last_user_message` | None (relies on `near_prompt_end` via `is_allowed`) |
| Prompt cache consumption | `states.erase(it_best)` (single-use) | Same |
| SWA checkpoint TODO | Present | Present |
